%index
CallNamedPipeW
Connects to a message-type pipe (and waits if an instance of the pipe is not available), writes to and reads from the pipe, and then closes the pipe. (CallNamedPipeW)
%group
Win32 kernel32
%prm
lpNamedPipeName, lpInBuffer, nInBufferSize, lpOutBuffer, nOutBufferSize, lpBytesRead, nTimeOut
lpNamedPipeName : [wstr] The pipe name.
lpInBuffer : [intptr] The data to be written to the pipe.
nInBufferSize : [int] The size of the write buffer, in bytes.
lpOutBuffer : [intptr] A pointer to the buffer that receives the data read from the pipe.
nOutBufferSize : [int] The size of the read buffer, in bytes.
lpBytesRead : [var] A pointer to a variable that receives the number of bytes read from the pipe.
nTimeOut : [int] The number of milliseconds to wait for the named pipe to be available. In addition to numeric values, the following special values can be specified.
%inst
Connects to a message-type pipe (and waits if an instance of the pipe
is not available), writes to and reads from the pipe, and then closes
the pipe. (CallNamedPipeW)

[戻り値]
If the function succeeds, the return value is nonzero. If the
function fails, the return value is zero. To get extended error
information, call GetLastError. If the message written to the pipe by
the server process is longer than nOutBufferSize, CallNamedPipe
returns FALSE, and GetLastError returns ERROR_MORE_DATA. The
remainder of the message is discarded, because CallNamedPipe closes
the handle to the pipe before returning.

[備考]
Calling CallNamedPipe is equivalent to calling the CreateFile (or
WaitNamedPipe, if CreateFile cannot open the pipe immediately),
TransactNamedPipe, and CloseHandle functions. CreateFile is called
with an access flag of GENERIC_READ | GENERIC_WRITE, and an inherit
handle flag of FALSE. CallNamedPipe fails if the pipe is a byte-type
pipe. Windows 10, version 1709: Pipes are only supported within an
app-container; ie, from one UWP process to another UWP process that's
part of the same app. Also, named pipes must use the syntax
`\\.\pipe\LOCAL\` for the pipe name.


%index
CallbackMayRunLong
Indicates that the callback may not return quickly.
%group
Win32 kernel32
%prm
pci
pci : [intptr] A pointer to a TP_CALLBACK_INSTANCE structure that defines the callback instance. The pointer is passed to the callback function.
%inst
Indicates that the callback may not return quickly.

[戻り値]
The function returns TRUE if another thread in the thread pool is
available for processing callbacks or the thread pool was able to
create a new thread. In this case, the current callback function may
use the current thread indefinitely. The function returns FALSE if
another thread in the thread pool is not available to process
callbacks and the thread pool was not able to create a new thread.
The thread pool will attempt to create a new thread after a delay,
but if the current callback function runs long, the thread pool may
lose efficiency.

[備考]
The thread pool may use this information to better determine when a
new thread should be created. The CallbackMayRunLong function should
be called only by the thread that is processing the callback. Calling
this function from another thread may cause a race condition. The
CallbackMayRunLong function always marks the callback as
long-running, whether or not a thread is available for processing
callbacks or the threadpool is able to allocate a new thread.
Therefore, this function should be called only once, even if it
returns FALSE. To compile an application that uses this function,
define _WIN32_WINNT as 0x0600 or higher.


